  "bad_tail_text": "NotImplementedError`. To fix this, we need to implement the logic to pop the highest priority job from the heap and return it. Additionally, we need to ensure that the `run_next` method is thread-safe by using a lock.\n\nHere is the updated `unicaa01_p01_panic/main.py` file:\n\nunicaa01_p01_panic/main.py\n```\n\"\"\"Broken priority scheduler \u2014 panic starter (stress_b style).\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport heapq\nimport threading\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\nfrom typing import Any\n\n\n@dataclass(order=True)\nclass Job:\n    priority: int\n    name: str = field(compare=False)\n    payload: dict[str, Any] = field(default_factory=dict)\n\n\nclass Scheduler:\n    def __init__(self) -> None:\n        self._heap: list[Job] = []\n        self._lock = threading.Lock()\n\n    def submit(self, job: Job) -> None:\n        with self._lock:\n            heapq.heappush(self._heap, job)\n\n    def run_next(self) -> Job | None:\n        with self._lock:\n            if self._heap:\n                return heapq.heappop(self._heap)\n            return None\n\n\ndef demo() -> list[str]:\n    s = Scheduler()\n    for p, n in [(2, \"backup\"), (1, \"alert\"), (3, \"email\")]:\n        s.submit(Job(p, n))\n    out: list[str] = []\n    for _ in range(3):\n        j = s.run_next()\n        if j is None:\n            break\n        out.append(j.name)\n    return out\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    result = demo()\n    print(result)\n```\n\nThis implementation ensures that the `run_next` method is thread-safe by using a lock, and it correctly pops the highest priority job from the heap. The `demo` function now prints `['alert', 'backup', 'email']` as expected.",
